<https://www.cyrilvoyant.com/scientific-publishing-trust#faq> a schema:FAQPage ;
    schema:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema:mainEntity [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "Not necessarily. What is missing is verification and recognition of editorial work, not more titles." ] ;
            schema:name "Do we need new journals?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "The number of indexed articles grew by about 47% between 2016 and 2022, faster than the number of researchers; unpaid peer review no longer keeps up, and more than 10,000 papers were retracted in 2023, a record." ] ;
            schema:name "Is scientific publishing really in trouble?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "Because it requires of high-risk AI systems what articles lack: data governance, technical documentation, record-keeping, a quality management system and post-market monitoring." ] ;
            schema:name "Why bring the AI Act into a discussion of articles?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "No: it should be declared. As with any other tool, authors state what was used and for what; what matters is that results are checked, data and code included." ] ;
            schema:name "Should AI be banned from writing and reviewing?" ] .

<https://www.cyrilvoyant.com/teaching/biostatistics/correlation-regression/#faq> a schema:FAQPage ;
    schema:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema:mainEntity [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "The asymptotic approximation can be poor; examine the size and structure of the table. A justified grouping or an exact method may be suitable, notably Fisher's test for a 2×2 table in its setting. Yates' continuity correction does not guarantee to solve the small-count problem." ] ;
            schema:name "What to do when the expected counts of a χ² are small?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "In simple linear regression with independent normal errors of constant variance: 95% confidence interval (CI) of the mean at x0 = ŷ0 ± t(n−2; 0.975) se √[1/n + (x0−x̄)²/Sxx], where ŷ0 is the value predicted by the line at x0 and t(n−2; 0.975) the Student quantile with n − 2 degrees of freedom. For a future observation, add 1 under the root. Here Sxx = Σ(xi−x̄)² and se² = Σresidual²/(n−2). The prediction interval therefore keeps the individual scatter; 1.96 is only an asymptotic approximation of the quantile." ] ;
            schema:name "What is the difference between a confidence interval and a prediction interval?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "Least squares weigh the square of the deviations: a point far away in x can strongly change the fitted slope and correlation, increasing or decreasing the apparent relationship depending on its position. Always look at the scatter plot and the residuals before concluding." ] ;
            schema:name "Why can a single outlier change everything?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "No: with 1000 pairs, r = 0.1 can be significant while explaining only 1% of the variation in a simple regression with an intercept. Rejecting ρ = 0 under a model proves neither causation nor predictive usefulness; the latter is also measured on data not used to fit the model." ] ;
            schema:name "Is a significant r enough to make a prediction useful?" ] .

<https://www.cyrilvoyant.com/teaching/biostatistics/descriptive-statistics/#faq> a schema:FAQPage ;
    schema:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema:mainEntity [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "The deviations are measured from the sample mean, which is as close to them as possible: dividing by n underestimates the population variance in expectation. Dividing by n − 1 corrects this bias, because one degree of freedom was used to estimate the mean." ] ;
            schema:name "Why divide by n − 1 and not by n?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "No: a large sample detects small departures and a small sample lacks power. One must look at the graphs, the extreme values, the independence and the inference question; the central limit theorem does not repair biased data collection or any dependence." ] ;
            schema:name "Is a normality test enough to choose a method?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "No. Under H0 and the assumptions of the model, it is the probability of a statistic at least as extreme as the one observed, for the chosen test. It is neither P(H0 | data) nor a measure of the practical importance of the effect. A small one only says that the data are poorly compatible with H0." ] ;
            schema:name "Does a p-value of 0.03 give the probability that H0 is true?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "For independent normal observations with an unknown variance, (x̄ − μ0)/(s/√n) follows exactly a Student distribution with n − 1 degrees of freedom (df), even for large n. Without normality, the approximation depends on the size and on the distribution; two-group and paired tests have their own conditions." ] ;
            schema:name "When is Student's distribution used for a mean?" ] .

<https://www.cyrilvoyant.com/teaching/biostatistics/probabilities/#faq> a schema:FAQPage ;
    schema:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema:mainEntity [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "With Se = Sp = 99% and a prevalence of 1/1000, among 1000 people one expects 0.99 true positives and 9.99 false positives, hence a PPV close to 9%. This illustrates Bayes' theorem; clinical management depends on context and protocol." ] ;
            schema:name "Why does prevalence change the predictive value?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "No. Youden's index and the distance to the ideal corner optimize different criteria; neither automatically encodes the clinical cost of errors. A decision needs, among other things, the prevalence and the respective consequences of false positives and false negatives." ] ;
            schema:name "Does the best threshold always maximize Se + Sp?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "For a score oriented toward the presence of disease, AUC = P(score of a sick person > score of a healthy person) + 0.5 P(tie). An AUC of 0.8 describes this ranking, not the individual probability of being sick, nor the quality at a given threshold." ] ;
            schema:name "What does an AUC of 0.8 mean?" ],
        [ a schema:Question ;
            schema:acceptedAnswer [ a schema:Answer ;
                    schema:text "It combines knowledge of the population (a prior distribution of the pharmacokinetic parameters) with the patient's own measurements: a few blood samples are enough for an individual estimate, which is refined with each new measurement." ] ;
            schema:name "Why is the Bayesian approach used to adjust a dose?" ] .

